//@ unimplemented "Line 7's blank now carries `@define printf(fmt: string, ...) <noret>;`, and that is a repair, 2026-08-27, under the owner's ruling that a sample whose own logic is wrong may be repaired rather than booked. Line 18 called printf while this file neither declared it nor imported it. Measured rather than recalled: sixteen samples call printf, fourteen of those declare or import it, and only this file and const.fin:68 were bare. The line added here is the corpus's own spelling, byte for byte identical at fourteen sites -- deeptest1.fin:4, deeptest2.fin:3, deeptest3.fin:3, enums.fin:5, functions.fin:3, lambdas.fin:5, letssee.fin:3, loops.fin:3, macros.fin:3, readonly.fin:5, simple_pointers.fin:3, stdlib/hashmap.fin:10, stdlib/stdio.fin:12, struct_methods.fin:3 -- so no text here is invented. Two counts are easy to confuse and both are true of different things: eighteen files carry an `@define printf` at all, the fourteen above plus complex.fin:5 and operators.fin:4 returning `<int>`, preprocessor.fin:3 with a parenthesised `<string>`, and literal_struct.fin:30, whose `@define printf()` sits inside `if (!@defined(\"printf\"))` and so declares nothing until the compiler API can evaluate that guard. The prelude question this repair rides on is still open. lib/std/stdio.fin:8-9 states the rule in force: \"Whether a sample has to import stdio to call printf is an owner ruling; today it does.\" This line obeys today's rule and settles nothing about tomorrow's -- if a prelude lands it becomes redundant rather than wrong, exactly as the other fourteen already would. The note this replaces blamed the search path (`src/driver/SearchPaths.hpp:107`) for printf being undefined here, and that was already false: importing.fin:4's `import { printf } from stdio;` resolves against the bundled library today, so the fault was the missing declaration and not where the compiler looked. The repair replaced a blank line rather than adding one, so every line number is what it was -- this file is cited at 4, 6, 8, 12, 16 and 25, and an insertion above the interface would have staled all six at once. One diagnostic is left and it is neither this file's nor the front end's: `item.to_string()` at 17:22 is `error: codegen: a call to the method 'to_string' on struct 'User' is not lowered yet`. The method call on the bounded type parameter `T` resolves -- a generic parameter's bound is stored on its GenericType and getStructType consults it, so the call finds Printable's method, where this sample reported `Type 'T' does not have methods` before that"
// 1. Define an Interface
interface Printable {
    pub fun to_string() <string>;
}
@define printf(fmt: string, ...) <noret>;
// 2. Define a Struct that "should" implement it (we don't check impl yet, just syntax)
struct User {
    name <string>,
    age <int>
}

// 3. Function with Generic Constraint
// T must implement Printable (checked in Semantics)
fun print_any<T: Printable>(item: T) <noret> { // an easier way to write this is : `fun print_any(item: Printable) <noret> {}`
    let s <string> = item.to_string();
    printf("%s", s);
}

fun main() <noret> {
    let u <User> = User { name: "Fin", age: 1 };
    // This would technically fail in a real compiler if User doesn't impl Printable
    // But for now, we just want to see if Semantics accepts the syntax.
    print_any::<User>(u);
}
